    {
      "id": "sticky_overview",
      "name": "Overview",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -48,
        0
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 1680,
        "height": 620,
        "content": "## 🗣️ Language Learning, Built for Real Life\n\nMade first for people who moved abroad for work and have to pick up the local language on the job - in meetings, hallway chats, emails from coworkers - not from a textbook. But it works just as well for anyone learning a language for travel, study, or just because.\n\nYou hear something new - a word, a correction, a phrase worth keeping - and by the time you think to write it down, it's gone. This workflow gives that moment somewhere to go, then makes sure you actually see it again.\n\n📝 **Capture** (below) turns anything you log into a real study card: AI looks it up and writes the meaning, an example sentence, and a grammar note, then saves it to your own Google Sheet - a vocabulary list built from what you actually say and hear at work and in daily life, not a generic textbook list.\n\n📬 **Digest** (below) is what makes it stick. Once a day it emails you one word from that sheet to review, cycling through everything you've logged so nothing gets forgotten. Consistency is most of what makes language learning work, and a short email that shows up on its own beats remembering to open an app.\n\nOnly one branch runs per execution, so each has its own Configuration node - just keep the Spreadsheet ID and Sheet Name the same in both (see the note on the right). Works for any language: just change the Target Language field."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "sticky_capture",
      "name": "Capture section note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -48,
        680
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 2,
        "width": 1680,
        "height": 420,
        "content": "## 📝 Capture a new word\n\nSubmit the form the moment you come across something worth remembering -> Configuration holds your target language, learner level, and sheet details -> Google Gemini looks up the term and returns a meaning, an example sentence, and a grammar breakdown -> Code cleans up the AI's response -> the row is appended to your Google Sheet as a permanent record, not a fleeting thought."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "sticky_digest",
      "name": "Digest section note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -48,
        1160
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 2,
        "width": 1676,
        "height": 420,
        "content": "## 📬 Daily word of the day\n\nOn a schedule you choose -> Configuration holds the same sheet details plus who to email -> every row in your vocabulary sheet is read -> Code picks the oldest word you haven't reviewed yet (and recycles through old ones once you've seen them all) -> Gmail sends it straight to your inbox -> the sheet is updated so it isn't repeated until everything's had a turn."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "sticky_sync_warning",
      "name": "Keep configs in sync warning",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1680,
        0
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 3,
        "width": 300,
        "height": 1580,
        "content": "## ⚠️ Keep both Configuration nodes in sync\n\nThe Spreadsheet ID and Sheet Name are duplicated in 'Set Language and Level' and 'Set Spreadsheet and Email' because only one branch runs per execution - they never pass data to each other directly. If you ever change or recreate the sheet, update both nodes, or one branch will silently write to (or read from) the wrong place."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
